R-LD2 R-LD3 R-UM1 R-AL4 R-PP2 R-PP3 R-PP4 R-P2 R-P3 R-P4 R-P5 R-P6 R-LD7 R-FL11 R-EP7 R-UP7 R-EC12 R-CC8 R-LF3 R-NS12 R-EF1 R-LY4 (slice 031, questions Q1 to Q5 of the slice): the unit probes of `Ladder.run!` (ladder step 1) with the fake probes. 19 runs of `update json` on 19 projects (acme/pf 0.1.0, acme/json 2.0.0 moves to 2.1.0, acme/parser 1.0.0). The user layer is `config.toml` `[probe] timeout = 9`. Each run has its own compiler id, because a fact is for a pair (hash, compiler) (R-P4). Made by `research/slice-031-revert/gen-un1.py` (the expected files by the reference).
The calls of a run that probes, in order: `Start.run!`, then one `db_query` for the keys of the packages (R-EF1, R-CC8), the copy, round 1 `probes N` (the baseline of the project and the unit checks of the packages that have no fact for this compiler, in the order pf, json, parser: R-PP2, R-PP3, R-AL4), one `db_write` with `existing=replace` (the facts of the round, before the verdict: R-PP4, R-CC8), then, when the units are fine, round 2 (the check of the candidate) and round 3 (the build). A unit probe is `check main.roc` in the dir of the package.
Run 1 (R-P6): the unit check of acme/parser fails in Parser.roc and in Yaml.roc, the app uses both, and the baseline has an error in Parser.roc: the baseline explains Parser.roc, so only Yaml.roc is blamed (exit 10, no candidate check). Run 2: the unit check fails in Parser.roc only, and the baseline has the same error: nothing is blamed (the unit fail is not new), the ladder goes on, the check passes with the note about the error that did not change, and the build passes. Run 3: the 3 units pass: 3 facts are written. Run 4: the same compiler: the facts are known, round 1 has the baseline only, and no write (R-AL4).
Run 5: json fails in Yaml.roc and the app does not use it: the fail is ignored (R-UM1), the fact is written (a fail with its module). Run 6: the app imports json.Yaml, and the fact of run 5 is known: the scan reads Yaml.roc and then Wire.roc, one `read_files` for each level, and the verdict names the module (exit 10, no candidate check). Run 7: a fail with no module of its hash (the error is in a file of another package): exit 10, `no module is named` (Q4 of slice 030). Run 8: a hang and a crash are facts, and a fault is a fail of the tuple (R-P3, R-EP7): 2 blames in the order of the nodes, the platform first.
Run 9: SIGKILL gives no fact (R-P3): exit 1, the 2 other facts are written. Run 10: warnings only is a pass (R-P2). Run 11 (R-P5, R-UP7, R-EC12, `probe.max = 3` of talon.toml): the baseline and 2 units fit, the third unit is cut: its facts are written for the 2 that ran, exit 12. Run 12 (`probe.max = 6`, same compiler): the 2 facts are known, round 1 has the baseline and the missing unit, and the run finishes. Run 13 (`--no-probe`, R-LF3): no new unit probe and no write. Run 14 (`--no-probe`): a fact that is known is judged, also with the flag (exit 10). Run 15 (`--slots 3`, R-FL11, and `probe = { slots = 5, timeout = 7 }` of talon.toml, R-LY4): the flag wins for the pool (`pool=3` in the log of each round), the talon.toml wins over config.toml for the timeout (7, not 9).
Run 16 (`probe.max = 4`) and run 17 (`probe.max = 5`): the baseline and the 3 units are 4 probes, so the check of the candidate has no probe left with 4 (exit 12, no unit left over); with 5 the check runs and the build has none. Run 18: a package project (kind `package`, no build): the scan starts at the exposed module `Lib` of the project dir and reads `Lib.roc`, then the files of the package that it imports (R-LD7). Run 19: a workspace of 2 apps: one `db_query` for the packages of both closures, then each member probes only the packages of its own closure that have no fact (member a: pf and json, member b: parser).
Slice 036b: the oracle loop probes the unit check of a changed release first, and the failure of the ladder goes back to the solver as a conflict (R-AL5, R-AL6). R-PH13: a P0 test of a failed ladder uses a case with no other tuple. So `talon.toml` of each project has the anchors `pf = "acme/pf@=0.1.0"`, `json = "acme/json@=2.1.0"` and `parser = "acme/parser@=1.0.0"` (p18 has the json anchor only). The result is the result of P0, with the line `note: tuple dropped: ...` before a failure of the ladder (a proposal, TW24) and the calls of the loop. Two differences in `tree.diff`: in run 7 (the hard fail of json 2.1.0) and in run 9 (SIGKILL of json 2.1.0) the loop stops at the unit probe of json, so the unit facts of pf and parser are not probed and not written. `probe/lp1-loop` shows the same cases with no anchors on json (the loop falls to json 2.0.5).
